schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: 'DAOs vs Traditional Organizations: A New Way to Coordinate'
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: comparison
  summary: >-
    Contrasts DAOs with traditional hierarchical firms across four dimensions —
    how decisions get made, organizational structure (flat vs pyramid), and how
    capital is raised (token issuance vs venture capital/loans/IPOs) — then
    lists benefits (reduced overhead, transparency, global access, aligned
    incentives) and honest challenges (voter fatigue, token concentration,
    legal uncertainty), with East-Africa-specific framing around cooperatives
    and cross-border participation.
  audience: Community-Steward
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      open-participation-for-anyone-with-a-token vs voter-fatigue-and-low-turnout
      — the article notes low participation is a real risk even though the
      option to participate is open.
    - >-
      wide-token-distribution-in-principle vs token-concentration-in-practice
      — "if a few people hold most tokens, governance can become centralized in
      practice."
  related_concepts:
    - flat-vs-hierarchical-organization
  related_resources:
    - DeepDAO
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/daos-vs-traditional.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-15f037f070f8
  source_lineage: daos-vs-traditional.md
  notes: >
    # assessed-but-excluded

    - No specific named tools beyond Snapshot (already covered as a resource in
      the dao-tooling.md ingest from this same batch) and DeepDAO (new here) —
      did not re-create a duplicate Snapshot resource object for this article.
    - No people/community profiles, exact locations, or high-risk triggers
      identified — general comparative/educational content; no
      public-use-boundary object created.
